.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii timer_getoverrun.2
.\"
.TH TIMER_GETOVERRUN 2 "July 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
timer_getoverrun \- get the overrun count of a POSIX per-process timer
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, -lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <time.h>
.PP
.BI "int timer_getoverrun(timer_t " timerid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR timer_getoverrun ()
returns the overrun count for the timer
.IR timerid ,
that is, the number of
.I additional
timer expirations that occurred between the time the last expiration signal
was generated and the time it was accepted or delivered.
.PP
Because a substrate signal is not queued more than once per signal number, a
periodic timer that expires while its previous notification signal is still
pending (for example because the signal is blocked) records each such extra
expiration as an overrun.  When that signal is finally accepted or delivered,
the accumulated count becomes the value returned by
.BR timer_getoverrun ().
A value of 0 means the notification was delivered before the next
expiration.
.PP
The returned value refers to the most recently delivered expiration signal.
Arming the timer with
.BR timer_settime (2)
resets the overrun count.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR timer_getoverrun ()
returns the timer overrun count (a non-negative integer).  On error, \-1 is
returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I timerid
is not a valid, live timer.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timer_create (2),
.BR timer_settime (2),
.BR timer_gettime (2),
.BR timer_delete (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008.
.SH NOTES
Timer expiry is sampled at the kernel tick, but the overrun count is computed
from the real elapsed time (using the timer's absolute nanosecond deadline),
so it reflects the number of interval periods that actually elapsed rather
than the number of ticks observed.
